<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Spanish Texas</h1><p class="st-meta">1690 CE – 1821 CE · capital San Antonio de Bexar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Spain's thinly settled frontier province of Tejas, founded to block the French, whose missions and ranches at San Antonio laid foundations for modern Texas.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Alarmed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-robert-cavelier-sieur-de-la-salle">La Salle's</a> French colony on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matagorda-bay">Matagorda Bay</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alonso-de-leon">Alonso de León</a> to east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, where the mission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> de los <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hasinai-confederacy">Tejas</a> was founded among the Hasinai <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/confederate-states-of-america">Confederacy</a> in 1690. It was abandoned in 1693, but French traders at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/natchitoches-louisiana">Natchitoches</a> prompted Spain to return in 1716, and in 1718 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-de-alarcon">Martín de Alarcón</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-antonio">San Antonio</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> was a military and missionary frontier of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain</a>. Franciscan missions, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamo-mission">San Antonio de Valero</a>, later known as the Alamo, gathered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coahuiltecan">Coahuiltecan peoples</a>, while presidios guarded the roads. From 1729 to 1772 the capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-adaes">Los Adaes</a>, near the French border; it then moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-antonio">San Antonio de Béxar</a>. Canary Islanders settled San Antonio in 1731, and ranching thrived. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a> raids constrained settlement until a peace with the Comanche in 1785.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The province suffered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-war-of-independence">Mexican War of Independence</a>, including a republican revolt crushed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> River in 1813. In 1821 it passed to independent <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mexico">Mexico</a>, and Anglo-American colonists under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-f-austin">Stephen F. Austin</a> began arriving, leading eventually to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-texas">Republic of Texas</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/spanish-texas.jpg" alt="Spanish Texas" width="320" height="401"><figcaption>Image: Stephen F. Austin / Henry Schenck Tanner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Austin_%26_Tanner_Map_of_Texas_with_Parts_of_the_Adjoining_States_1836_UTA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1690 CE – 1821 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>San Antonio de Bexar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Martín de Alarcón, Marqués de Aguayo, Antonio María Martínez</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Province of Texas, Tejas</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1756&amp;focus=spanish-texas" aria-label="Spanish Texas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2114 1102 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Spanish Texas lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2434 1358L2321 1717M2434 1358L2321 1717"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2321 1717h0M2321 1717h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2434 1358h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2434 1358h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1756&amp;focus=spanish-texas">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
